# Documentation governance

Important architectural and behavioral knowledge must live in the repository,
not only in chat history or agent memory.

## Before significant work

Read the relevant source of truth first:

1. `AGENTS.md` — global invariants
2. Accepted ADRs in `docs/adr/` — architectural decisions
3. `docs/crawler/` — crawler contracts and store playbooks
4. Matching `.cursor/rules/` — operational agent rules
5. Code — resolve conflicts in favor of accepted ADR + current behavior

## Documentation impact review (required before finishing significant changes)

Ask: did this change introduce or alter any of the following?

- architecture / module boundaries / public contracts
- critical invariant or fallback policy
- pricing or availability semantics
- attribute source priority / title fallback
- error classification (`ParseError` vs `UPSTREAM_BLOCKED` vs OOS)
- blocking / challenge / CAPTCHA / auth-wall resolution / retry / session / concurrency behavior
- fetch / Camoufox / proxy policy (only when explicitly allowed)
- store-specific durable behavior
- environment variable, endpoint, deployment, or security requirement
- important operational limitation

If **yes** → update the **canonical** doc in the **same task**.
If **no** (trivial fix, local refactor, selector tweak with same semantics) →
do **not** invent documentation.

If the change **leaves unfinished work**, also follow
`.cursor/rules/pending-work.mdc` and add/update `docs/pending/PENDING-*`.
Technical blockers require prior investigation per
`.cursor/rules/research-and-problem-solving.mdc` (preserve research notes in
the pending item).

If the change **fully resolves** a pending item: remove it from the active
index, delete or archive the file, and clean related TODOs in the same task
(`.cursor/rules/task-completion.mdc`).

Final user-facing reports must be in pt-BR
(`.cursor/rules/language-pt-BR.mdc`).

## Where to write (single source of truth)

| Knowledge | Canonical location |
|---|---|
| Global agent invariants | `AGENTS.md` (short; link out) |
| Architectural decision + rejected alternatives | `docs/adr/` (new ADR; never silently rewrite Accepted) |
| Crawler contracts / errors / attributes / pricing norms | `docs/crawler/contracts.md` |
| Store-specific durable behavior | `docs/crawler/stores/<store>.md` |
| Agent workflow / skills / proxy / fetch immutability / research-before-block / performance | `.cursor/rules/` |
| Skills inventory | `docs/skills/README.md` |
| Actionable incomplete work | `docs/pending/` |
| Performance budgets / slow ops | `docs/performance.md` |
| Operator overview | `README.md` |

Prefer updating an existing document over creating a parallel copy.
Other files may **link** to the canonical doc; do not paste four full copies
of the same rule.

## ADR rules

Create an ADR when the decision:

- affects architecture or boundaries;
- creates a durable rule with meaningful trade-offs;
- spans modules or will likely be re-litigated;
- rejects important alternatives.

Do **not** create an ADR for broken CSS selectors, typo fixes, or local refactors.

Never edit an Accepted ADR to flip its decision silently. Supersede with a new
ADR and update `docs/adr/README.md`.

## Store docs

Document non-obvious durable behavior only (sources, pricing/availability
semantics, blocking, invariants, limitations). Do not paste implementation
line-by-line. Never document secrets, cookies, tokens, or credentials.

## Final report

When docs change, mention which files were updated and why in the task summary.
